.TH "valgrind4hpc" "1" "2018-10-08" "" ""
.hy
.SH NAME
.PP
\f[B]Valgrind4hpc\f[R]
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
valgrind4hpc \f[B][VALGRIND4HPC OPTIONS]\f[R] [-n \f[I]number of
ranks\f[R] ] \f[I]executable\f[R] [-- \f[B][EXECUTABLE ARGUMENTS]\f[R]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
\f[B]Valgrind4hpc\f[R] is a Valgrind-based debugging tool to aid in the
detection of memory leaks and errors in parallel applications.
Valgrind4hpc aggregates any duplicate messages across ranks to help
provide an understandable picture of program behavior.
Valgrind4hpc manages starting and redirecting output from many copies of
Valgrind, as well as deduplicating and filtering Valgrind messages.
If your program can be debugged with Valgrind, it can be debugged with
Valgrind4hpc.
.SH OPTIONS
.PP
\f[B]-n\f[R], \f[B]--num-ranks\f[R]=\f[I]RANKS\f[R]
.PD 0
.P
.PD
Run with this number of ranks (default 1)
.PP
\f[B]-t\f[R], \f[B]--tool\f[R]=\f[I]TOOL\f[R]
.PD 0
.P
.PD
Run memcheck, helgrind, exp-sgcheck, or drd with this tool on the
backend (default is memcheck)
.PP
\f[B]-l\f[R], \f[B]--launcher-args\f[R]=\f[I]\[dq]arguments\[dq]\f[R]
.PD 0
.P
.PD
arguments to the application launcher (WLM-specific)
.PP
\f[B]-i\f[R], \f[B]--inputfile\f[R]=\f[I]FILE\f[R]
.PD 0
.P
.PD
Use file as input to all ranks
.PP
\f[B]-o\f[R], \f[B]--outputfile\f[R]=\f[I]FILE\f[R]
.PD 0
.P
.PD
Specify an output file
.PP
\f[B]-s\f[R], \f[B]--suppressions\f[R]=\f[I]FILE\f[R]
.PD 0
.P
.PD
Specify a suppression file (can specify this argument multiple times)
.PP
\f[B]--gen-suppressions\f[R]=\f[I][yes|no]\f[R]
.PD 0
.P
.PD
Generate Valgrind suppressions for this job run.
By default, print output to standard error at the end of the job.
To specify a file, set \f[B]--gen-suppressions-file\f[R].
.PP
\f[B]--gen-suppressions-file\f[R]=\f[I]FILE\f[R]
.PD 0
.P
.PD
Write generated suppressions to this file.
If \f[B]--gen-suppressions\f[R] is not set, this option will enable it.
.PP
\f[B]-v\f[R], \f[B]--valgrind-args\f[R]=\f[I]\[dq]arguments\[dq]\f[R]
.PD 0
.P
.PD
Specify non-Valgrind4hpc-supported Valgrind arguments such as
--show-leak-kinds or --leak-check.
Note that certain arguments may interfere with the functionality of
Valgrind4hpc.
.PP
\f[B]-r\f[R], \f[B]--from-ranks\f[R]=\f[I]<ranks>\f[R]
.PD 0
.P
.PD
Only show Valgrind output from certain ranks.
Format: \[dq]a-c,i-k\[dq] to show output from ranks \[dq]a\[dq] through
\[dq]c\[dq] and \[dq]i\[dq] through \[dq]k\[dq].
.PP
\f[B]-g\f[R], \f[B]--vgdb-error\f[R]=\f[I]<count>\f[R]
.PD 0
.P
.PD
Start VGDB mode upon encountering this number of errors.
Valgrind4hpc will print connection instructions to run on the node for
the target rank.
Direct SSH access to nodes is required for interactive GDB debugging.
.PP
\f[B]--cray-pmi\f[R]=\f[I]OPTION\f[R]
.PD 0
.P
.PD
ALPS only: disable automatic MPI check and manually specify whether the
target application is a parallel MPI program.
Note: this solves an ALPS-specific problem and is not necessary on SLURM
systems.
Possible values: {yes, no}
.PP
\f[B]-h\f[R], \f[B]--help\f[R]
.PD 0
.P
.PD
Display help text and exit
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
To run the program \f[B]./a.out\f[R] and debug it across 32 ranks on 16
nodes with full Valgrind leak-checking, use the command:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
valgrind4hpc -n32 --launcher-args=\[dq]-N16 -j2\[dq] --valgrind-args=\[dq]--track-origins=yes --leak-check=full\[dq] ./a.out -- arg1 arg2
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Note that valgrind4hpc and target program arguments should be separated
by two dashes, \f[B]--\f[R]
.SH FALSE NEGATIVES
.PP
Valgrind needs to know what memory-allocating functions to wrap.
To this end, Valgrind4hpc informs Valgrind which shared library symbols
perform memory-allocating functions.
To function correctly, target executables must be built dynamically and
contain debug symbols.
When calling the Cray compiler, set the environment variable
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
CRAYPE_LINK_TYPE=dynamic
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
and compile with the debug symbol flag \f[B]-g\f[R]
.SH VALGRIND SUPPRESSIONS
.PP
For a full guide, see the Valgrind manual section 2.5: \[dq]Suppressing
errors\[dq] at <http://valgrind.org/docs/manual/valgrind_manual.pdf>.
.PP
The Valgrind option \f[B]--gen-suppressions\f[R]=\f[I]yes\f[R] will
automatically generate a suppression file for the currently-running
program.
If you would like to use this option with Valgrind4hpc, remember to pass
it as a custom Valgrind option with
\f[B]--valgrind-args=\[dq]--gen-suppressions\f[R]=yes\[dq]
.PP
When you have created your custom suppression file, you can pass it to
Valgrind4hpc with the \f[B]--suppressions\f[R]=\f[I]filename\f[R]
argument.
